these people are working to safeguard  democracy in lebanon ahead of the  elections on sunday they are making the  final preparations to ensure that the  electoral process is fair and  transparent  and that's not an easy task in a deeply  polarized country where sectarian  loyalty is the perhaps the leading  driving force for people when casting  their wards  the lebanese association for democratic  elections or lady is largely funded by  european countries  diana al-baba is responsible for  coordinating and overseeing all  activities of the association  and she believes that corruption is also  hurting democracy in lebanon  we have some sort of clientelistic  relationship between the voters and  their mps and their candidates  if i give you money you have to vote for  me  or i don't give you this money i can  provide you a service by being by  inserting you by appointing you  in a certain position in the public  sector  if you don't vote for me  i will you'll not get this job  lebanon has been subjected to chronic  political instability for the last  several years that led to large-scale  demonstrations against the political  elite ultimately forcing the government  to resign  in the following year a devastating  explosion at port of beirut killed 2018  people further deteriorating the already  crippled economy and deepening the  political instability  the elections on sunday are being seen  by many as a litmus test for real change  and affords are on the way to avoid  fraud violations and irregularities  the minister of interior is in charge of  ensuring the transparency of these  elections  we trust the lebanese people and our  security forces we will observe every  detail during the election day it is our  people's right to have a transparent  election that their voice and decision  is the ultimate power the law will be in  place for those who try to violate the  rules  however there is a deep mistrust in  state institutions including the  judiciary as most people believe they  are all deeply pulsized  despite a series of measures taken by  the interior ministry serious concerns  remain about possible violations of the  electoral process people here say there  is no real deterrence mechanism to  prevent irregularities and as in lebanon  political parties have rarely been held  accountable for their actions there is  little hope that this time will be  different  al jazeera beirut
 

